            <ArticleTitle>Paroxysmal neuropathic pain in an adolescent female with syringomyelia: a review of literature.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>472-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/PEC.0b013e318253907f</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Paroxysmal neuropathic pain is an uncommon complaint among pediatric patients visiting the emergency department. It is a rare presentation in children with syringomyelia. Patients with syringomyelia may present with a variety of pain symptoms. It is the site and extension of the syrinx, which determines the character of pain. We report an adolescent with Chiari malformation type 1 with syringomyelia who presented with neuropathic pain, dysesthesia, and absent triceps (C7) reflex. The pertinent literature is reviewed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
